; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Every Possible Order
; CATEGORY: Recursion
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     recursion, permutations, lists, anagrams, map
; SUMMARY:  Lists every way to arrange some items, and uses it to find anagrams of a word and to seat friends at a table.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   How many ways can three friends stand in a line? To find them all,
;   pick who goes first, then line up the other two in every possible
;   way. Lining up the other two is the same problem, only smaller.
;   PERMS outputs a list of all the orders. The program prints them for
;   a short list, counts them for longer ones, and finds all the
;   arrangements of the letters in a word.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   1. Press Run and read the results.
;   2. Put a breakpoint on the first line of PERMS,
;        if emptyp :items [output [[]]]
;      and run with F5. In the Call Stack, PERMS and WITH.FIRST take
;      turns: each PERMS has a list one item shorter than the one
;      before. Press F5 and watch :items and :choice in the Variables
;      panel.
;   3. Try changing the word given to ANAGRAMS at the bottom.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * building every arrangement by choosing a first item
;   * MAP and FPUT to put the chosen item in front of many lists
;   * removing just one copy of an item, and REMDUP for repeated answers
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Compare the counts with the factorials in factorial.lgo. Then add
;     7 and 8 to the list of sizes and notice how long it takes.
;   * Try print anagrams "boot. Why do some answers appear twice?
;   * Make a list of four colors and draw a striped flag for each order.
; ============================================================

; The only way to arrange nothing is the empty arrangement, so the
; base case outputs a list holding one empty list. Getting this right
; is what makes everything above it work.
to perms :items
  if emptyp :items [output [[]]]
  output map.se [with.first ? :items] :items
end

; All the orders that start with :choice. Take :choice out, arrange
; what is left in every way, and put :choice back on the front of each.
; REMOVE takes out every copy, so for letters that repeat we remove
; just the first one with WITHOUT.
to with.first :choice :items
  output map [fput :choice ?] perms without :choice :items
end

to without :x :items
  if emptyp :items [output []]
  if equalp :x first :items [output butfirst :items]
  output fput first :items without :x butfirst :items
end

to anagrams :w
  ; turn the word into a list of letters, permute, glue back together
  output map [apply "word ?] perms letters :w
end

to letters :w
  if emptyp :w [output []]
  output fput first :w letters butfirst :w
end

print [Three friends can stand in a line in these ways:]
foreach perms [Ana Ben Cy] [print ?]
print []
foreach [1 2 3 4 5 6] [
  print (sentence ? [items can be arranged in] count perms iseq 1 ? [ways])
]
print []
print [All the orders of the letters in "tea:]
print anagrams "tea
print [The letters of "boot, with the doubled o, give repeats.]
print [REMDUP throws the copies away:]
print remdup anagrams "boot
